{"kind":"stack","id":"fossils-1","title":"How fossils are formed","theme":{"background":"#f5efe0","foreground":"#2b2b2b","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"What is a fossil?"},{"type":"image","picto":"book"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a","label":"The remains or traces of something that lived long ago","why":"Category: a kind of record. A fossil is the remains or traces of a living thing preserved in rock.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"A rock that is made by a volcano","why":"Category: a kind of rock. Pupils mix fossils up with igneous rock, which forms from cooled lava.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-c","label":"A plant that grows in the sea","why":"Category: a living thing. Pupils mix fossils up with living seaweed, which is not preserved.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Sediment"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"Small bits of sand, mud or soil that settle in layers, often at the bottom of rivers, lakes or seas."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Where do most fossils form?"},{"type":"image","picto":"river"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"In layers of sediment at the bottom of water","why":"Category: a place. Most fossils form when remains are buried in sediment under water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"On the tops of tall mountains","why":"Category: a place. Pupils think high ground is where old things are, but mountains are not where sediment settles.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Inside the core of a volcano","why":"Category: a place. Pupils forget that the heat inside a volcano would destroy remains, not preserve them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"What happens to a plant or animal after it dies in a river or lake?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"It is covered by sediment before it rots away","why":"Category: a process. Burial by sediment protects remains from decay so they can become fossilised.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"It floats on the surface until it dries out","why":"Category: a process. Pupils forget that remains must be buried quickly to survive.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"It turns into soil straight away","why":"Category: a process. Pupils mix up decay with fossilisation, which needs burial first.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"You find a shell in a rock on a beach. Do you take it home to show your teacher, or leave it where it is for others to see?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"Take it home","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"Leave it in the rock","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You take the shell home and show your teacher. Your teacher says it is a fossil, and it will help scientists learn about the past."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"You leave the shell in the rock. Later, a scientist visits the beach and studies the fossil in its rock where it was found."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Why are fossils found in rock?"},{"type":"image","picto":"rain"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Because the remains were trapped in layers of sediment that became rock","why":"Category: a reason. Sediment is pressed and cemented into rock, trapping the remains inside.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Because the rock grew around the animal while it was alive","why":"Category: a reason. Pupils think rock grows, but rock forms from sediment after burial.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Because the animal turned into a stone when it died","why":"Category: a reason. Pupils imagine the body changes straight into stone, but it is buried in sediment first.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-t","content":"Mould fossil"},{"type":"text","id":"c9-e","content":"A hollow shape left in rock when a body part decays away after it has been buried."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which part of a living thing is most often preserved as a fossil?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Hard parts such as bones, shells and teeth","why":"Category: a body part. Hard parts resist decay, so they are the parts most often preserved.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Soft parts such as skin and muscle","why":"Category: a body part. Pupils forget that soft parts usually rot before they can be fossilised.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Blood and water","why":"Category: a body substance. Pupils forget that liquids drain or evaporate and cannot leave a fossil.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"You have finished the fossil lesson!"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Well done. Fossils tell us about living things from long ago, trapped in rock for millions of years."}],"actions":[]}}
